**Argument (Repeated readiness and full-byte comparison)** · `arg:cas:wire-ordering-scaling`

`take_ready` visits all remaining records after every assignment, charging one Work for each visit: exactly `n*(n+1)/2`, namely 666, 2,556 and 3,486 scans for the measured 36, 71 and 83 records. A not-yet-ready record retries `key` for each dependency through its first missing dependency, re-encoding synthetic keys on every attempt; this accounts for 5,266, 42,980 and 63,874 Work, about 61 %, 82 % and 84 % of ordering (`ex:cas:wire-scalar-sealing`), (`ex:cas:wire-grid-sealing`), (`ex:cas:wire-skew-sealing`). Ready descriptors are encoded once and retained (`test:unit:ready-descriptor-reuse-keeps-exact-bytes`), but minimum selection still compares complete encoded records; there is no additional Work charge per compared byte. Unordered dependency sorts instead precharge their member count times the parent's payload length, independently of actual comparison count; no certificate-row sorting occurs in these three fixtures. Descriptor comparisons can therefore grow with common-prefix length even when charged Work does not. The observed ordering costs per symbolic payload byte are 7.94, 18.31 and 19.66; the exact quadratic scan count and repeated key construction explain why this route remains an optimization obligation. Any replacement must preserve full structural ordering, including child-reference order and late payload differences (`test:unit:ready-reference-order`), (`test:unit:ready-descriptor-reuse-separates-content`). A measured indexed alternative, the argument that it preserves this order and the conditions for adopting it are (`ex:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-costs`), (`arg:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-equivalence`), (`req:cas:wire-indexed-ordering`).

