Work with both eight- and sixteen-byte prefixes; its test stops at that first inventory, so the rise is a lower bound on the small-table cost.

**Argument (Indexed readiness preserves the canonical order)** · `arg:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-equivalence`

The scan route of (`arg:cas:wire-ordering-scaling`) assigns at each step the least complete descriptor among records whose references and optional space are all assigned, takes the first in local key order among byte-identical candidates, and coalesces byte-identical records into one index. An indexed route reproduces that sequence when four properties hold. A record enters the heap exactly when its last distinct child is assigned, so the heap holds the scan route's ready set at every step; counting certificate-row members as children changes nothing for current records, because each member is a reference of the cover statement that its application references and is therefore assigned first. Heap comparisons order exactly as complete descriptor bytes: unequal prefixes decide, equal prefixes compare interned word trees whose node identities are exact child pairs rather than digests, and byte length decides a common prefix. Ties fall back to local key order; this matters even though identical records coalesce, because the record assigned first decides which of its parents become ready first. Unordered sets and certificate rows sort by the same exact comparison with a stable sort, preserving child-reference order and late payload differences (`test:unit:ready-reference-order`), (`test:unit:ready-descriptor-reuse-separates-content`). Comparing the two routes on sealed tables, as in (`ex:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-costs`), checks these properties only on the tables a test suite reaches: it cannot expose a rule both routes share, such as the tie order, nor any refusal path, because a refusal publishes no table.

**Requirement (Indexed ordering adoption)** · `req:cas:wire-indexed-ordering`

Canonical ordering keeps the scan route of (`arg:cas:wire-ordering-scaling`) until a replacement meets the conditions below. Resolving each dependency once while keeping the scan selection is a separable first step: the scan route already computes every dependency key on each record's successful readiness attempt, so one resolution pass charges no more Work on any table and leaves the bytes unchanged, and on the measured witnesses it replaces 5,266, 42,980, 44,861 and 63,874 Work of repeated readiness keys with 225, 1,012, 1,012 and 1,287 (`ex:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-costs`). The heap and compact keys add the remaining measured saving only after five conditions hold. Memory: a complete simultaneous retained-memory bound for sealing covers deep source payloads, every retained descriptor copy, interning tables at capacity, adjacency lists, the heap and temporary framing and sorting buffers within the allowance of (`arg:cas:wire-footprint-scaling`), as (`cav:cas:wire-retained-memory-audit`) requires; the partial census already counts 37,180 of the scalar witness's 42,432 allowance bytes. Pins: every Work pin and literal `N`/`N−1` boundary in the workspace is measured to completion, and falling figures are updated in the same change with their old and new values; a rise is admitted only with a per-table attribution to fixed heap, key and notification cost or to key preparation that charges comparison work the scan route performs uncharged, and the first inventory rise of (`ex:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-costs`) is only a lower bound. Charges: a metered sort keeps a comparator that is a total order on every path, so Work is charged before or after a pure sort and never refused inside it; certificate-row reordering charges at least the bytes it copies out and back, and unordered-set sorts charge their element copies, amending (`arg:cas:wire-ordering-scaling`). Verification: byte and index identity with the scan route on every table the complete suite seals, including set and row permutations; a Work-limit sweep through every charged unit of ordering, on tables with sets large enough to leave the standard sort's small-input path, that refuses without panic or partial output; and byte-identical records under distinct keys, with the existing tie order preserved; the comparison runs as a test, not behind debug assertions or environment variables (`arg:cas:wire-indexed-ordering-equivalence`). Structure: no measurement observer or environment read remains in production code, and link construction, seeding, assignment and notification stay separate functions within the 100-line limit.

